WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.470 --> 00:00:09.520
Every video game should be awesome, but...we
know that’s not the case.

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:15.810
So, we’ll usually just settle for video
games at least being functional.

00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:22.300
Unfortunately, many video games ship with
glitches that make them impassable, or just

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:23.539
outright ridiculous.

00:00:23.539 --> 00:00:30.380
Here are a few bugs that destroyed perfectly
good games, and should have sent their programmers

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:32.710
looking for new work.

00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:33.710
Ducked up

00:00:33.710 --> 00:00:38.670
It's a simple game about hunting delicious
birds for sport, but even Nintendo’s Duck

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:40.980
Hunt was published with a game-breaking glitch.

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:47.630
If you can endure your snickering dog for
99 levels of bird-murdering fun, that's it.

00:00:47.630 --> 00:00:48.860
There's nothing there.

00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:55.110
Your reward is "Round 0," a foul purgatory
where immortal birds disappear and reappear

00:00:55.110 --> 00:00:56.370
at random.

00:00:56.370 --> 00:01:02.930
The game ends, proving that there's no reward
for duck genocide, in real life or in Nintendo.

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:08.400
Now for the real challenge: loading those
thousands of duck corpses into your pickup

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:09.400
truck.

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:10.810
The missing Pokemon

00:01:10.810 --> 00:01:16.390
In the olden days, there were only 151 Pokémon,
and that was good enough.

00:01:16.390 --> 00:01:22.140
Because of programming limitations, the original
Game Boy games left spots for 256 different

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:24.880
encounters in Pokémon Red and Blue.

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:31.440
151 slots were filled with Pokémon, others
with trainers, and 39 spots were left empty.

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:37.140
If you follow a specific sequence of actions,
you'll encounter the mess of code called "MissingNo"

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:39.100
and be able to capture it.

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:44.580
But doing so can cause game-breaking errors
in other parts of your save.

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:47.100
Sometimes, you shouldn't catch 'em all.

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:48.350
Crappy holidays

00:01:48.350 --> 00:01:53.930
Back in the day, Sony's video game magazine,
PlayStation Underground, would regularly send

00:01:53.930 --> 00:02:00.159
out preview discs to their subscribers, but
around Christmas of 2004, PlayStation 2 owners

00:02:00.159 --> 00:02:03.570
were in for a pretty terrible surprise.

00:02:03.570 --> 00:02:10.560
Unfortunately, the PlayStation Underground
Holiday 2004 Demo disc would destroy the save

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:16.510
data on any memory card still plugged into
its system, regardless of the game.

00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:19.490
Sony sent out warnings, but it was too late.

00:02:19.490 --> 00:02:21.080
“Stop, don’t, come back”

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:25.790
Gamers lost thousands of hours of playtime
data when their memory cards were blanked

00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:26.790
out.

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:31.480
For many players, those hours had been invested
in the recently-shipped Grand Theft Auto:

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:33.630
San Andreas.

00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:39.920
Just think of all those un-murdered civilians.

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:41.010
Boss loss

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:47.069
Bubble Bobble Revolution on the Nintendo DS
includes one of the dumbest bugs ever released.

00:02:47.069 --> 00:02:52.090
What should have been a simple remake of a
hundred-level game was accidentally cut short

00:02:52.090 --> 00:02:58.069
at Level 30, for no other reason than the
programmers forgot to put in a boss.

00:02:58.069 --> 00:03:03.379
Because the boss battle was an essential trigger
to move on to Level 31, the game was simply

00:03:03.379 --> 00:03:04.870
unbeatable.

00:03:04.870 --> 00:03:10.629
Codemasters eventually replaced the bugged
carts, but they’ll never live down the unmitigated

00:03:10.629 --> 00:03:13.810
duncery of their mistake.

00:03:13.810 --> 00:03:15.599
Object overload

00:03:15.599 --> 00:03:20.629
The massive glitch found in The Elder Scrolls
4: Shivering Isles is also pretty simple:

00:03:20.629 --> 00:03:24.690
if you play too much of this Oblivion expansion,
your game will crash.

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:29.709
Because of a programming issue, the PC version
of the expansion would freeze once its memory

00:03:29.709 --> 00:03:31.940
was full of background data.

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:36.440
This was because that data wasn’t being
recycled when it was no longer in use.

00:03:36.440 --> 00:03:42.489
Bethesda pushed out a fix after about a month,
and the irony that a game expansion called

00:03:42.489 --> 00:03:48.989
Shivering Isles had severe freezing issues
was lost on no one.

00:03:48.989 --> 00:03:51.290
Don't change a thing

00:03:51.290 --> 00:03:56.980
The SoulCalibur series includes a pretty solid
string of fighting games, but a terrible bug

00:03:56.980 --> 00:04:03.060
in the third installment nearly derailed the
whole thing for PlayStation 2 users.

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:07.459
The error doesn't manifest as part of the
game itself, but rather when players attempt

00:04:07.459 --> 00:04:13.650
to change, move, copy, or delete any of their
older save data associated with any their

00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:14.650
games.

00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:20.100
The glitch can potentially destroy or corrupt
all of the data on a player's memory card.

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:24.940
While gamers have been able to figure out
workarounds, like saving on a separate memory

00:04:24.940 --> 00:04:30.110
card for just this one game, no amount of
jiggle physics is worth losing all of your

00:04:30.110 --> 00:04:31.110
games.

00:04:31.110 --> 00:04:32.110
Infinitely easy

00:04:32.110 --> 00:04:37.800
Players may furiously debate whether or not
the introduction of "infinite spin" to Tetris

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:43.620
Worlds was a glitch or not, but it's game-breaking
enough to be considered a travesty either

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:44.620
way.

00:04:44.620 --> 00:04:49.600
By repeatedly hitting the "spin" button, a
player can keep a Tetris piece in the air

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:53.120
indefinitely, which is a totally cheap move.

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:57.830
The official Tetris company unconvincingly
denies that the inclusion of this cheater

00:04:57.830 --> 00:05:03.440
move is a glitch, but the easy spin was made
optional in subsequent games.

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:07.170
But still, it’s not the worst thing to ever
happen to Tetris.

00:05:07.170 --> 00:05:10.280
That credit goes to appearing in Pixels.

00:05:10.280 --> 00:05:11.570
No signal

00:05:11.570 --> 00:05:17.009
Batman already has it tough enough, but in
Batman: Dark Tomorrow, he’s pretty much

00:05:17.009 --> 00:05:18.550
doomed from the start.

00:05:18.550 --> 00:05:25.340
The only way to really beat villain R'as al
Ghul is to find a signal device hidden somewhere…

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:29.790
Except the game never tells you where it is,
or that it even exists.

00:05:29.790 --> 00:05:36.130
While it's one of the most Kafkaesque video
game twists of all time, no one plays video

00:05:36.130 --> 00:05:39.570
games to feel like a giant, helpless bug.

00:05:39.570 --> 00:05:46.479
And making a game with no direction about
how to actually win is a pretty big bug, indeed.

00:05:46.479 --> 00:05:47.479
Faceoff

00:05:47.479 --> 00:05:54.190
Ubisoft should've taken its time to give Assassin's
Creed Unity a proper debut as the first next-gen

00:05:54.190 --> 00:05:56.000
exclusive of the series.

00:05:56.000 --> 00:06:01.391
Instead, it was rushed out the door with a
plethora of glitches, clipping issues, and

00:06:01.391 --> 00:06:07.650
animation hiccups, which is a bad look considering
the countless number of Assassin's Creed titles

00:06:07.650 --> 00:06:09.050
before it.

00:06:09.050 --> 00:06:14.440
Even after the patches, Unity still has more
bugs than the courtesans of Paris.

00:06:14.440 --> 00:06:15.850
The worst offense?

00:06:15.850 --> 00:06:22.490
A glitch during cutscenes that caused faces
to disappear, resulting in a horrifying, grotesque

00:06:22.490 --> 00:06:28.530
visage of hair, eyeballs, teeth, and gums
floating above characters' clothes.

00:06:28.530 --> 00:06:33.470
We hope someone at Ubisoft got the guillotine
for deciding to ship this ugly, unfinished

00:06:33.470 --> 00:06:34.560
product.

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:36.260
Every part of Superman 64

00:06:36.260 --> 00:06:42.150
When a game starts out by covering everything
in a "Kryptonite fog" because it can't render

00:06:42.150 --> 00:06:46.850
its own graphics, you know you're off to a
bad start.

00:06:46.850 --> 00:06:52.320
Regarded as one of the worst games of all
time, Superman: The New Adventures found an

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:58.600
audience among small, stupid children, but
is so full of game-crushing glitches that

00:06:58.600 --> 00:06:59.900
it's considered unplayable.

00:06:59.900 --> 00:07:05.840
Here’s the catch, though: The whole game
takes place in a virtual reality programmed

00:07:05.840 --> 00:07:11.889
by Lex Luthor, so maybe Lex is just a really,
really terrible programmer.

00:07:11.889 --> 00:07:17.060
From that perspective, this is actually the
most clever game of all time.

00:07:17.060 --> 00:07:18.060
Thanks for watching!

00:07:18.060 --> 00:07:21.870
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:07:21.870 --> 00:07:26.159
And leave us a comment to let us know which
video game bugs drove you crazy...

